module nm-criminal-trial-court.criminal

// Felony cases in the New Mexico district courts under NMRA Rule Set 5
// (2025 edition). lit/core has no counting rule for Rule 5-104, so every
// period is stated in a note, not encoded as a counted deadline. Outcomes
// are from the defendant's side. The earlier outline's leading decisions
// (County of Riverside v. McLaughlin, Gerstein v. Pugh, Gideon v.
// Wainwright, Brady v. Maryland, Mapp v. Ohio, Barker v. Wingo, Boykin v.
// Alabama, Missouri v. Frye, Jackson v. Virginia, Ramos v. Louisiana,
// Apprendi v. New Jersey, Roe v. Flores-Ortega, Garza v. Idaho) are
// removed: no reporter text is vendored; Rule 5-301's committee commentary
// quotes County of Riverside v. McLaughlin.
start arrested

state arrested: "Arrest, summons or complaint" by applicant cite "NMRA 5-201"
{
  note "A prosecution is commenced by filing a complaint, an information or an indictment (NMRA 5-201(A)); warrants and summonses issue under Rule 5-208. After an arrest without a warrant, a judge determines probable cause promptly, and in any event within 48 hours after custody begins and no later than the first appearance, whichever is earlier; the court may not extend the 48 hours, and weekends and legal holidays count notwithstanding Rule 5-104(A) (NMRA 5-301)."
  edge-arrested-appearance "First appearance and release" -> initial-appearance {
    authority cite "NMRA 5-301"
  }
}

decision initial-appearance: "Release or detention" by court cite "NMRA 5-401"
{
  note "For a case initiated in the district court whose release conditions the magistrate or metropolitan court has not set, the district court holds a hearing and sets conditions of release as soon as practicable, and no later than three days after arrest for a defendant held in the local detention center, five days for one held elsewhere, or by arraignment for a defendant not in custody (NMRA 5-401(A)(1)). For a felony, the prosecutor may move for an expedited pretrial detention hearing at any time, and the court may order detention if the state proves by clear and convincing evidence that no release conditions will reasonably protect any person or the community (NMRA 5-409(A), (B)); the hearing commences no later than five days after the later of the motion's filing and the resulting arrest (NMRA 5-409(F)(1)(a)). Release and detention orders may be appealed under Rule 5-405."
  edge-initial-appearance-detained "Detained on the state's motion" -> probable-cause {
    authority cite "NMRA 5-409"
  }
  edge-initial-appearance-released "Released on conditions" -> probable-cause {
    authority cite "NMRA 5-401"
  }
}

decision probable-cause: "Preliminary examination or grand jury" by court cite "NMRA 5-302"
{
  note "A preliminary examination is held, with a disposition entered, within a reasonable time and no later than 10 days if the defendant is in custody or 60 days if not, counted from the latest of the first appearance and the other events Rule 5-302(A)(1) lists; on good cause the court may extend the limits by up to 60 days, and without the defendant's consent only on a record showing of exceptional circumstances (NMRA 5-302(A)). On a finding of no probable cause, the court dismisses those felony charges without prejudice and discharges the defendant (NMRA 5-302(D)(1)). A case may instead proceed by grand jury indictment (NMRA 5-302.2)."
  edge-probable-cause-charged "Bound over; information or indictment filed" -> charged {
    authority cite "NMRA 5-302(D)"
  }
  edge-probable-cause-none "No probable cause; defendant discharged" -> dismissed {
    authority cite "NMRA 5-302(D)"
  }
}

state charged: "Indictment or information" by office cite "NMRA 5-201"
{
  note "An information is a written statement signed by the district attorney containing the essential facts and the common name of the offense (NMRA 5-201(C)). The defendant is arraigned on the information or indictment within 15 days after its filing or the arrest, whichever is later (NMRA 5-303(A))."
  edge-charged-arraignment "Arraignment" -> arraignment { authority cite "NMRA 5-303" }
}

state arraignment: "Arraignment and plea of not guilty" by applicant cite "NMRA 5-303"
{
  note "Arraignment is on the information or indictment within 15 days after its filing or the arrest, whichever is later; the defendant may appear by two-way audio-visual communication as Rule 5-303(A) allows (NMRA 5-303(A)). The pleas are those Rule 5-304(A) allows. Disclosure by the state and by the defendant are governed by Rules 5-501 and 5-502."
  edge-arraignment-discovery "Discovery" -> discovery { authority cite "NMRA 5-501" }
}

state discovery: "Discovery and pretrial motions" by applicant cite "NMRA 5-601"
{
  note "Unless the rules or the court provide otherwise, a pretrial motion is made at arraignment or within 90 days after it, unless the court waives the time on good cause (NMRA 5-601(E)(1)). Defenses and objections based on defects in the initiation of the prosecution or in the charging document (other than jurisdiction or failure to charge an offense) must be raised before trial or are waived, though the court may grant relief for cause (NMRA 5-601(D)). A party intending to call witnesses at an evidentiary hearing on a motion discloses them at least five days before the hearing unless the court orders a shorter time (NMRA 5-601(F)). For a case of concurrent jurisdiction refiled from a lower court, the defendant may move for dismissal if trial is not set within the lower court's deadline, on the factors Rule 5-604(B) lists; for cases originally filed in the district court the rule sets no deadline (NMRA 5-604(C))."
  edge-discovery-motions "Pretrial motion filed" -> motions-ruling {
    authority cite "NMRA 5-601(E)"
  }
  edge-discovery-speedy "Motion to dismiss for violation of the right to a speedy trial" -> motions-ruling {
    authority cite "NMRA 5-604(B)"
  }
  edge-discovery-plea "Plea negotiations" -> plea-decision { authority cite "NMRA 5-304(E)" }
}

decision motions-ruling: "Ruling on pretrial motions" by court cite "NMRA 5-601(G)"
{
  note "All motions are disposed of within a reasonable time after filing (NMRA 5-601(G)). If a defense or objection to the initiation of the prosecution or the charging document is sustained and not otherwise remediable, the court dismisses the complaint, indictment or information (NMRA 5-601(D))."
  edge-motions-ruling-speedy "Dismissed for violation of the right to a speedy trial" -> dismissed {
    authority cite "NMRA 5-604(B)"
  }
  edge-motions-ruling-dismissed "Charges dismissed on another ground" -> dismissed {
    authority cite "NMRA 5-601(D)"
  }
  edge-motions-ruling-denied "Motions denied; case proceeds" -> plea-decision {
    authority cite "NMRA 5-601(G)"
  }
}

decision plea-decision: "Plea or trial" by applicant cite "NMRA 5-304"
{
  note "A plea agreement that would eliminate the need for trial is submitted by a deadline the court sets, and no plea agreement may be entered later than five days before the scheduled date for jury selection or the start of a bench trial; the court sets a hearing within the plea deadline at which the defendant must be present (NMRA 5-304(E)). An untimely plea agreement is governed by Rule 5-304(F)."
  edge-plea-decision-plea "Plea accepted" -> sentencing { authority cite "NMRA 5-304(C)" }
  edge-plea-decision-trial "Trial" -> trial { authority cite "NMRA 5-605(A)" }
}

decision trial: "Verdict" by court cite "NMRA 5-611(A)"
{
  note "Cases required to be tried by jury are so tried unless the defendant waives a jury with the court's approval and the state's consent (NMRA 5-605(A)). The verdict is unanimous, signed by the foreman and returned in open court (NMRA 5-611(A))."
  edge-trial-acquittal "Not guilty" -> acquitted { authority cite "NMRA 5-611(A)" }
  edge-trial-guilty "Guilty; post-verdict motions" -> post-trial { authority cite "NMRA 5-614" }
}

decision post-trial: "Post-verdict motions" by court cite "NMRA 5-614"
{
  note "A defendant may move for or renew a motion for judgment of acquittal within 14 days after the jury returns a guilty verdict or the court discharges the jury, whichever is later (NMRA 5-614.1(B)). A motion for a new trial on a ground other than newly discovered evidence is made within ten days after the verdict or finding of guilty, or further time the court fixes within those ten days; one on newly discovered evidence may be made before final judgment or within two years after it (NMRA 5-614(C)). Failure to move for a new trial does not waive error properly brought to the court's attention (NMRA 5-614(E)). A Rule 5-614 motion filed not later than 30 days after the judgment is filed restarts the time for appeal from the order disposing of it (NMRA 12-201(D))."
  edge-post-trial-acquittal "Judgment of acquittal" -> acquitted { authority cite "NMRA 5-614.1" }
  edge-post-trial-new-trial "New trial granted" -> trial { authority cite "NMRA 5-614(A)" }
  edge-post-trial-denied "Motions denied; sentencing" -> sentencing {
    authority cite "NMRA 5-701(A)"
  }
}

state sentencing: "Judgment and sentence" by court cite "NMRA 5-701(A)"
{
  note "If the defendant is found guilty and the court determines that sufficient evidence supports the verdict, a judgment of guilty is rendered; the judgment and sentence are rendered in open court and a written judgment and sentence is signed and filed (NMRA 5-701(A)). When imposing or deferring sentence after a trial on a not-guilty plea, the court advises the defendant of the right to appeal and to appeal at state expense if unable to pay (NMRA 5-702(A))."
  edge-sentencing-judgment "Written judgment and sentence filed" -> judgment {
    authority cite "NMRA 5-701(A)"
  }
}

decision judgment: "Judgment of conviction" by applicant cite "NMRA 5-702"
{
  note "Within the time the Rules of Appellate Procedure allow, defense counsel files either a notice of appeal under Rule 12-201 or the defendant's sworn affidavit, in the approved form and witnessed by counsel, stating the decision not to appeal (NMRA 5-702(B)). A notice of appeal is filed within 30 days after the judgment or order appealed from is filed in the district court clerk's office (ten days for the state's appeal of a suppression order), and Rule 12-308(B)'s three added days do not apply (NMRA 12-201(A)). A sentence imposed in an illegal manner may be corrected within 30 days after it is imposed, and a motion to reduce a sentence may be filed within 90 days of the written judgment and sentence or the other events Rule 5-801(B) lists; a motion to reduce tolls the time for appeal only if filed within 30 days of the written judgment and sentence (NMRA 5-801(A)-(C)). lit/core has no counting rule for the appellate rules, so the period is not encoded as a counted deadline."
  edge-judgment-appeal "Notice of appeal filed" -> appeal-taken { authority cite "NMRA 12-201(A)" }
  edge-judgment-no-appeal "No appeal" -> conviction-final { authority cite "NMRA 12-201(A)" }
}

terminal acquitted: "Acquitted" outcome [win] cite "NMRA 5-701(A)"
{
  note "Outcome from the defendant's side: a verdict of not guilty, a judgment of acquittal or a judgment of not guilty (NMRA 5-611, 5-614.1, 5-701(A))."
}

terminal dismissed: "Charges dismissed" outcome [win, dismissal] cite "NMRA 5-601(D)"
{
  note "Outcome from the defendant's side. A dismissal for want of probable cause at the preliminary examination is without prejudice (NMRA 5-302(D)(1)); whether another dismissal bars refiling depends on its ground."
}

terminal appeal-taken: "Appeal taken" outcome [appeal] cite "NMRA 12-201(A)"
{
  note "The appeal continues under the Rules of Appellate Procedure (lit/nm-state-appellate; sources/nm-state-appellate)."
}

terminal conviction-final: "Conviction final" outcome [loss] cite "NMRA 5-802"
{
  note "Outcome from the defendant's side. State habeas corpus (NMRA 5-802) and post-sentence relief (NMRA 5-803) are not encoded here; federal habeas belongs to lit/habeas."
}
